# 3. Non-negotiable architecture principles

## 3.1 One source of truth per business domain

Existing CORE domain ownership remains canonical:

| Domain | Canonical owner |
|---|---|
| Identity and tenancy | CORE-001 |
| Events and event delivery | CORE-002 |
| Lead ownership and attribution | CORE-003 |
| Project/property/unit inventory | CORE-004 |
| Search and matching | CORE-005 |
| Deals and transactions | CORE-006 |
| Trust, fraud and governance | CORE-007 |
| Commission and financial truth | CORE-008 |

The TypeScript API must not reimplement these business engines.

The API layer owns request validation, authentication, trusted context, response contracts and orchestration. Existing CORE modules remain responsible for domain decisions and invariants.

## 3.2 Approved technology target

Follow ARCH-037 and ARCH-038. Do not silently substitute a technology or add an alternative framework.

| Layer | Approved target |
|---|---|
| Public API | TypeScript, Node.js 24, Fastify 5.x |
| API protocol | Versioned REST and typed JSON contracts |
| Persistent system of record | PostgreSQL 18.4+ |
| Geospatial capability | PostgreSQL/PostGIS |
| Cache and ephemeral coordination | Redis 8 |
| Durable event streams | Kafka |
| Long-running workflows | Temporal |
| Object/document storage | S3-compatible storage |
| Vector retrieval | Qdrant |
| AI runtime | Existing Python AI platform and approved AI Gateway |
| Model providers | Approved OpenAI and vLLM fallback configuration |
| Background processing | Approved Node workers and durable Temporal workflows |

Any proposed version or provider change must be checked against the current approved architecture and recorded through the authorized decision process.

## 3.3 Preserve existing Python systems

Preserve and reuse:

- `AUTONOMY_ENGINE/core/`
- `AUTONOMY_ENGINE/ai_platform/`
- `AUTONOMY_ENGINE/security/authentication/`
- `AUTONOMY_ENGINE/runtime/`
- `AUTONOMY_ENGINE/continuity/acrl/`
- Existing execution and evidence modules.

The Node.js application cannot directly import Python modules. A private, versioned, authenticated Python domain-runtime integration boundary is required.

Use private REST/JSON contracts consistent with the approved API architecture. Select the concrete Python HTTP hosting dependency only after inspecting the existing dependency set and recording the decision through the authorized architecture process.

The Python runtime must never be exposed as an unauthenticated public business API.

## 3.4 Data and event ownership

- PostgreSQL is authoritative business truth.
- Redis is not permanent business storage.
- Kafka is the durable event-stream transport.
- Temporal is the durable long-running workflow engine.
- Qdrant and other indexes are derived projections.
- Analytics and AI summaries are not canonical business records.
- ACRL is development continuity and recovery infrastructure, not a customer business-domain owner.

## 3.5 Automation authority

AI may interpret, propose and assist. It may not grant itself permissions.

Every mutation must pass server-side identity, tenant, authorization, policy and idempotency checks.

Payment, settlement, commission release, legally binding commitments, ownership changes and other irreversible/high-impact operations must require the applicable human approval.

## 3.6 Backend/frontend ownership boundary

Backend owns:

- API request and response contracts.
- OpenAPI semantics.
- Authentication and authorization enforcement.
- Tenant and resource isolation.
- Business rules and state transitions.
- Persistence and data integrity.
- Server-side AI tool permissions.

Frontend owns:

- Visual presentation and interaction.
- Client-side form state and UX validation.
- Rendering backend-owned data.
- Calling documented APIs.
- Displaying backend authorization and workflow outcomes.

Frontend must not become a second business engine or authorization authority.

## 3.7 No duplicate platform rule

Do not introduce another:

- Identity or tenant system.
- CORE business engine.
- Financial ledger.
- Event bus.
- Workflow engine.
- AI gateway.
- Usage ledger.
- Customer history store.
- Evidence engine.
- State store.
- ACRL continuity layer.

A new component is allowed only when source inspection proves that no suitable existing owner exists and the architecture decision is authorized.

