## 5.1 H360-01 — Search criteria

The current search filter source may be narrower than the full intended customer search contract.

The canonical typed search contract must be extended through CORE-004/CORE-005 ownership to support, where approved source data exists:

- Property type.
- Bedroom/BHK count.
- Budget minimum and maximum.
- Currency.
- Area range and measurement unit.
- City and locality.
- Transaction type.
- Project and inventory identity.
- Lifecycle and availability.
- Geospatial coordinates and radius where supported by canonical location data.
- Versioned criteria and normalization semantics.

Unsupported, ambiguous, malformed or contradictory criteria must be reported, clarified or rejected. They must never be silently discarded.

## 5.2 H360-02 — Customer AI API

Implement one versioned, authenticated public AI request boundary.

Every request must include a trusted server-side identity and tenant context, validated locale, bounded prompt, approved conversation reference, request ID and idempotency policy.

The API must:

- Validate input and output schemas.
- Apply rate limits and cost quotas before expensive provider calls.
- Call the existing AI platform using a private runtime adapter.
- Return source-bound citations.
- Redact secrets and internal exceptions.
- Enforce tenant context and authorization.
- Handle timeout and provider failure safely.

No customer-facing AI feature may be claimed live until an actual API integration and E2E route test succeeds.

## 5.3 H360-03 — Listing truth and grounded retrieval

Every material listing claim must be traceable to an authorized, current source record.

The system must detect:

- Stale listings.
- Conflicting sources.
- Revoked or inaccessible evidence.
- Missing inventory fields.
- Search index lag.
- Unsupported legal or financial claims.
- Prompt injection in listing text or uploaded documents.

AI-generated citations are not accepted unless they resolve to real retrieved source records.

## 5.4 H360-04 — Customer context and privacy

Define:

- Conversation ownership and tenant scope.
- Persistent versus ephemeral context.
- Retention period and deletion policy.
- Consent and permitted use.
- User access and export.
- Legal-hold handling where applicable.
- Summarization and context minimization.
- PII/secret redaction.
- Audit access records.

The existing local JSON context cache and evidence ledger are not the authoritative customer conversation or business database.

## 5.5 H360-05 — Multilingual and Pune quality

Build a versioned evaluation corpus for:

- English.
- Hindi.
- Marathi.
- Transliteration and mixed-language queries.
- Pune locality and neighbourhood names.
- Landmark and locality aliases.
- Local real-estate terminology.
- Property-type and BHK expressions.
- Ambiguous locations.
- Price, area and currency interpretation.

Use human-reviewed examples and record language-specific search and AI-quality metrics.

## 5.6 H360-06 and H360-07 — Governed autonomous execution

The action lifecycle must be:

`REQUEST → AUTHENTICATE → RESOLVE TENANT → AUTHORIZE → VALIDATE POLICY → CHECK IDEMPOTENCY → PLAN → APPROVAL CHECK → EXECUTE → VERIFY → RECORD EVIDENCE → CLOSE OR RECOVER`

AI may produce a plan or action proposal, but it cannot authorize itself.

Use Temporal for durable long-running workflows and Kafka for durable event delivery. Use transactional outbox/inbox and stable idempotency keys.

Do not claim exactly-once messaging. Achieve effectively-once business outcomes through durable deduplication and reconciliation.

## 5.7 H360-08 — Identity and tenant security

